*C0003
BSRN-Payerne file for June 2016 (format renewed in 2018).

06/03/2016:
Large discrepancy between SW global1 and global2, but impossible to 
determine what is the correct measurement.

06/2015:
Data from the 10m SWup pyranometer is of bad quality (pyranometer may be 
dirty) from beginning of the month to the 09 June.

05/2015:
Data from the 10m SWup pyranometer is of bad quality (pyranometer may be 
dirty) from 07 May to the end of the month.

04/2015:
Missing mast data (30m) from 24 to 27 April and 28, 29 April, as well as 10m 
data on 28, 29 April. This seems to be related to a logger problem and all 
data from the given levels are missing.

11/2014:
Missing data on 24 and 25 November (afternoon and night data) for SW direct 
2m, SW diffuse 2m and LW 2m downward (sun tracker data acquisition system 
problem, LW is also on sun tracker because it is shaded). This also has a 
small influence on SW global data: because the LW data are missing, the 
corresponding SW data on these days could not be corrected for thermal 
offset. These SW data are included, but they are not corrected for thermal 
offset. It should be kept in mind that the thermal offset correction is 
small; generally 5Wm-2 or less.

09/2014:
Some missing (invalidated) data on various mornings during the month for SW 
diffuse 2m (problem of shading because of non-optimal sun tracker setting).

07/2014:
Missing data on 5 and 7 July for SW direct 2m, SW diffuse 2m and LW 2m 
downward (sun tracker data acquisition system problem, LW is also on sun 
tracker because it is shaded). This also has a small influence on SW global 
data: because the LW data are missing, the corresponding SW data on these 
days could not be corrected for thermal offset. These SW data are included, 
but they are not corrected for thermal offset. It should be kept in mind 
that the thermal offset correction is small; generally 5Wm-2 or less.

02/2014:
See below comment for January 2014 on LW 2m downward data problem also 
extending up to the 03/02/2018.

01/01/2014:
LW 2m downward data are missing (invalidated) from the 23/01/2014 to the 
03/02/2018. This also has a small influence on SW data: because the LW data 
are missing, the corresponding SW data on these days could not be corrected 
for thermal offset. These SW data are included, but they are not corrected 
for thermal offset. It should be kept in mind that the thermal offset 
correction is small; generally 5Wm-2 or less.

01/12/2013:
Some LW data are missing on the 5/11/2013, 6/11/2013 7/11/2013 and 8/11/2013 
(not continuously only some on each day). This also has a small influence on 
SW data: because the LW data are missing, the corresponding SW data on these 
two days could not be corrected for thermal offset. These SW data are 
included, but they are not corrected for thermal offset. It should be kept 
in mind that the thermal offset correction is small; generally 5Wm-2 or 
less.

01/11/2013:
Some LW data are missing on the 05/11/2013 and on the 14/11/2013. This also 
has a small influence on SW data: because the LW data are missing, the 
corresponding SW data on these two days could not be corrected for thermal 
offset. These SW data are included, but they are not corrected for thermal 
offset. It should be kept in mind that the thermal offset correction is 
small; generally 5Wm-2 or less.

01/04/2013:
Same problem as in March: some sun trackers pointing problems affecting the 
quality of some SW direct measurements that have consequently been 
invalidated.

01/03/2013:
Some sun trackers pointing problems affecting the quality of some SW direct 
measurements that have consequently been invalidated.

01/02/2013:
A large number of data from the sun trackers are invalid due to malfunction 
of the data acquisition electronics. This includes the SW direct and diffuse 
as well as the LW down at 2m (shaded instrument) and the UVB diffuse.

01/01/2013:
All tower data (10m and 30m LW and SW upward and downward data) are invalid 
until December 3. In addition a significant number of data are invalid for 
other parameters.
Logical record L1000 (SYNOP) is written by a new software. The result is 
different than with the previous software version. Elements that can be 
checked relatively easily tends to show the previous version was making 
errors and the new version seems more correct.

01/12/2012:
All tower data (10m and 30m LW and SW upward and downward data) are invalid 
from December 29.

01/11/2012:
10m and 30m (tower) LW upward data as well as 30m LW downward data are 
invalid until November 15.

01/10/2012:
SW direct measurements invalid from 3 to 16 October. All 30m (tower) LW 
downward data are invalid.

01/09/2012:
All 30m (tower) LW downward data are invalid.

01/06/2012:
Significant number of SW direct invalid data especially before June 7, and 
several days where SW diffuse data where contaminated because of 
deficiencies in the shading.

01/02/2012:
Situation similar to 01/2011 with problem in diffuse sun tracker alignment 
generating higher than usual number of bad (invalidated) SW diffuse data.

01/01/2012:
Situation similar to 12/2011, but a problem in diffuse sun tracker alignment  
generated higher than usual number of bad (invalidated) SW diffuse data.

01/12/2011:
Situation improving with respect to 10 and 11/2011 with fewer parameters 
showing higher than usual number of bad (invalidated) data and SW direct 
becoming available from 09/12/2011.

01/11/2011:
Situation similar to 10/2011 with number of bad (invalidated) data higher 
than usual and SW direct not yet available.

01/10/2011:
Data acquisition resumed for most parameters after refurbishing the BSRN 
station. However, the number of bad (invalidated) data is higher than usual 
for many of the recovered parameters and SW direct is not available this 
month yet.

01/09/2011:
Data acquisition still for refurbishing the BSRN station. Consequently the 
data is missing for most parameters (except tower measurements) for the 
whole month.

01/08/2011:
Data acquisition on most parameters was stopped on August 15 for 
refurbishing the BSRN station. Consequently the data is missing for most 
parameters (except tower measurements) for the second half of the month.

01/06/2011:
Data submission was resumed after a very long interruption using a new 
automatic station to archive file generation system. In addition, thermal 
offset corrections were introduced for short-wave global, diffuse and even 
reflected measurements, as well as calibration corrections derived from 
comparisons with the absolute cavity radiometer.

01/04/2010:
LW downward measurements at 2m (quantity no 5) are not anymore performed by 
an instrument (pyrgeometer Eppley PIR 28806F3) on the 2m bench with 
equatorial shading system, but with an instrument on a sun-tracker driven 
shading system (pyrgeometer Eppley PIR 31962F3).

01/07/2006:
Diffuse radiation data (SW diffuse, quantity no 4 and UV diffuse, quantity 
no 124) are measured with instruments on a new shading system on a sun 
tracker. The difference with the old measurements is a significant 
improvement in the determination of diffuse radiation. However, this will 
introduce a jump in the time series, which will have to be homogenized. 
Consequently, the influence of the imperfection of the old shading system 
will have to be estimated, the old data homogenized and resubmitted.
The instrument measuring diffuse SW radiation on the new system is a Kipp & 
Zonen pyranometer of type CM22, S.N. 030077, and the instrument measuring UV 
erythemal diffuse radiation is a broadband SolarLight UVB radiometer of type 
SL501A, S.N. 3551

24/08/2004:
The instruments on the 30m tower were put back in operation. All radiation 
instruments as well as meteorological instruments on the tower have been 
replaced, and pyrgeometers were upgraded with EPPLEY PIR with 3 dome 
thermistors. The data acquisition system on the tower was also completely 
renewed.

21/06/2004:
The instruments on the 30m tower were disconnected for a major upgrade of 
the tower equipment. The upgrade will last at least until end of July.

01/05/2004:
Due to a shortage of manpower, the instruments ON THE 30m TOWER did not 
undergo the regular maintenance in May. In particular, some dirt was present 
on the pyranometer at 30m. Thus, there are differences between the SW 
downward global flux measured at 2m and 30m that are most likely due to dirt 
on the sensor at 30m.

01.01.2002:
The short-wave direct irradiance is measured with CH1 from Kipp & Zonen. 
Using monthly samples over the period 01/2000 -> 09/2003, the median 
difference between CH1 and NIP stays within about 5 W/m2, while 80 percent 
of the distribution (10 to 90th percentile) is within 20 W/m2. (There are 
only 2 months where the 10th or 90th percentiles of the differences are on 
the order of -20 or W/m2. Usually these percentiles are around -10 and W/m2 
respectively.) In relative term, the 10th percentile of the CH1 to NIP ratio 
is between 0.95 and 0.99, the median of the ratio is between 0.99 and 1.01, 
while the 90th percentile of the ratio is between 0.99 and 1.03.
Even though the differences are sometime large, there does not seem to be an 
important bias. The percentiles are quite variable, but the overall median 
difference is about 1.5 W/m2 for the whole period 01/2000 -> 09/2003. These 
results are obtained by constantly adjusting the NIP to the PMO6 absolute 
radiometer (online calibration), while the CH1 does not need recalibration 
and compares well with the PMO6. Actually, the CH1 agrees slightly better 
with the PMO6 than the "PMO6-adjusted" NIP.

01.01.2002:
The BSRN site scientist for the Payerne station is Laurent Vuilleumier.

01.01.2001:
Laurent Vuilleumier was hired at MeteoSwiss and given responsibility for the 
radiation data. The period November to June 2002 (BSRN workshop in Regina) 
is a transition period where responsibility as BSRN scientist will be 
transferred from Alain Heimo to Laurent Vuilleumier. Data transfers to WRMC 
were late, and starting from the files for August 2001, the monthly quality 
checks were performed by Laurent Vuilleumier who also assembled the files to 
be transferred to WRMC.

01.01.2001:
The BSRN project has been put in operational mode within MeteoSwiss and 
placed under the responsibility of the Atmospheric Data group (Dr. P. 
Viatte, Email: pierre.viatte@meteosuisse.ch). This means that changes in the 
organization of the Payerne station will occur on short term. For the time 
being, the "old" staff remains the same, ad interim.

01.08.2000:
At the end of the month a new sun-tracking system has been installed on the 
station's facility. This new system will provide better pointing accuracy 
for the instruments. The old SPM sun-photometer (3 wavelengths) has been 
replaced by a new Precision Filter Radiometer PFR standard with nominal 
wavelengths at 368, 412, 500 and 862 [nm].

01.01.1999:
Sun-tracker system was out of order from 25.9.1998 to 12.1.1999; all direct 
radiation measurements were therefore set to default values.

01.02.1998
UV measurements are included (log. rec. 0500) since February 1998.

01.01.1997:
Major field upgrade at 2 meter level performed on the 9/1/97.

Unknown date:
Format changes as described in your E-mails from 21/6/96 and 2/7/96.

